unmet_need:
A neutral, shared record that makes the split of responsibility between a looping patient and their clinician explicit and documented, so clinicians can engage within current professional guidance without implying they endorse or prescribe an unregulated system. Today clinicians fear that any settings advice makes them responsible for an unapproved algorithm, while legal analysis says the real exposure is a narrow one: inaccurate statements and undocumented advice. Nothing records:
(a) what the patient was told and accepted (unregulated, own risk, data-protection caveats), in the consensus panel 3 format;
(b) the exact system the patient was running when advice was given (app and version, algorithm, pump and warranty or recall status, CGM type);
(c) whether each settings change was suggested by the clinician, a peer or the user, and who applied it;
(d) a single route to report adverse events to the clinic, the developers and the regulator.

Clinicians also lack a short, up-to-date, citable brief showing that major bodies (the 2022 consensus with ADCES, ISPAD and IDF; Diabetes Canada 2023; ADA 2025) now expect support rather than refusal. Without it, the 2019 FDA warning and the Medtronic recall keep acting as a default reason to disengage, and patients keep hiding their use.
